Drop the timing-sensitive `pipe-output` test.

The test compares gzip's exit status with cat's when both write to an
already-closed pipe (`... | :`) after a `sleep 0.01`, a SIGPIPE race upstream
itself flags as timing-sensitive (the "cat/grep: write error: Broken pipe"
lines are the test's own expected writes). On a loaded native runner the two
sides can land on different statuses and it fails; it lost the race on
aarch64. The other 29 tests run, and the shipped binary is unaffected (tests/
is not compiled in).

Makefile.in is patched after Makefile.am so it never looks older and automake
is not re-run.

--- a/tests/Makefile.am
+++ b/tests/Makefile.am
@@ -26,7 +26,6 @@
   memcpy-abuse				\
   mixed					\
   null-suffix-clobber			\
-  pipe-output				\
   reproducible				\
   stdin					\
   synchronous				\
--- a/tests/Makefile.in
+++ b/tests/Makefile.in
@@ -1868,7 +1868,6 @@
   memcpy-abuse				\
   mixed					\
   null-suffix-clobber			\
-  pipe-output				\
